          "type": "user",
          "text": "     1→# Messages\n     2→\n     3→Messages form the backbone of communication in the AG-UI protocol, representing conversation history between users and AI agents while maintaining compatibility across different AI providers.\n     4→\n     5→## Message Types\n     6→\n     7→The system defines six message roles:\n     8→\n     9→### User Messages\n    10→\n    11→Support both plain text and multimodal content (images, audio, files):\n    12→\n    13→```typescript\n    14→interface UserMessage {\n    15→  id: string;\n    16→  role: \"user\";\n    17→  content: string | MultimodalContent[];\n    18→}\n    19→```\n    20→\n    21→### Assistant Messages\n    22→\n    23→Can include optional text and tool calls:\n    24→\n    25→```typescript\n    26→interface AssistantMessage {\n    27→  id: string;\n    28→  role: \"assistant\";\n    29→  content?: string;\n    30→  toolCalls?: ToolCall[];\n    31→}\n    32→```\n    33→\n    34→### System Messages\n    35→\n    36→Provide instructions or context:\n    37→\n    38→```typescript\n    39→interface SystemMessage {\n    40→  id: string;\n    41→  role: \"system\";\n    42→  content: string;\n    43→}\n    44→```\n    45→\n    46→### Tool Messages\n    47→\n    48→Contain execution results linked to specific tool calls:\n    49→\n    50→```typescript\n    51→interface ToolMessage {\n    52→  id: string;\n    53→  role: \"tool\";\n    54→  toolCallId: string;\n    55→  content: string;\n    56→}\n    57→```\n    58→\n    59→### Activity Messages\n    60→\n    61→Structured progress updates rendered by frontends:\n    62→\n    63→```typescript\n    64→interface ActivityMessage {\n    65→  id: string;\n    66→  role: \"activity\";\n    67→  activity: ActivityData;\n    68→}\n    69→```\n    70→\n    71→### Developer Messages\n    72→\n    73→Internal debugging communication:\n    74→\n    75→```typescript\n    76→interface DeveloperMessage {\n    77→  id: string;\n    78→  role: \"developer\";\n    79→  content: string;\n    80→}\n    81→```\n    82→\n    83→## Streaming Architecture\n    84→\n    85→Messages support three-phase streaming for real-time responsiveness:\n    86→\n    87→```typescript\n    88→// Phase 1: Start\n    89→{ type: \"TEXT_MESSAGE_START\", messageId: \"msg1\", role: \"assistant\" }\n    90→\n    91→// Phase 2: Content chunks\n    92→{ type: \"TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT\", messageId: \"msg1\", delta: \"Hello \" }\n    93→{ type: \"TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT\", messageId: \"msg1\", delta: \"there!\" }\n    94→\n    95→// Phase 3: End\n    96→{ type: \"TEXT_MESSAGE_END\", messageId: \"msg1\" }\n    97→```\n    98→\n    99→## Tool Integration\n   100→\n   101→Tool calls are embedded in assistant messages, with results returned via tool messages:\n   102→\n   103→```typescript\n   104→// Assistant message with tool call\n   105→{\n   106→  id: \"msg1\",\n   107→  role: \"assistant\",\n   108→  toolCalls: [{\n   109→    id: \"tc1\",\n   110→    name: \"get_weather\",\n   111→    arguments: { location: \"London\" }\n   112→  }]\n   113→}\n   114→\n   115→// Tool result message\n   116→{\n   117→  id: \"msg2\",\n   118→  role: \"tool\",\n   119→  toolCallId: \"tc1\",\n   120→  content: JSON.stringify({ temperature: 18, conditions: \"cloudy\" })\n   121→}\n   122→```\n   123→\n   124→This creates a traceable chain of actions between the agent and tools.\n   125→\n   126→## Synchronization\n   127→\n   128→Two mechanisms handle message sync:\n   129→\n   130→### Messages Snapshot\n   131→\n   132→Complete snapshot for initialization and major state changes:\n   133→\n   134→```typescript\n   135→{\n   136→  type: \"MESSAGES_SNAPSHOT\",\n   137→  messages: [\n   138→    { id: \"1\", role: \"user\", content: \"Hello\" },\n   139→    { id: \"2\", role: \"assistant\", content: \"Hi there!\" }\n   140→  ]\n   141→}\n   142→```\n   143→\n   144→### Streaming\n   145→\n   146→Ongoing interactions use the three-phase streaming pattern for real-time updates.\n   147→\n   148→## Multimodal Content\n   149→\n   150→User messages can include various content types:\n   151→\n   152→```typescript\n   153→interface MultimodalContent {\n   154→  type: \"text\" | \"image\" | \"audio\" | \"file\";\n   155→  // Type-specific fields\n   156→}\n   157→\n   158→// Example with image\n   159→{\n   160→  id: \"msg1\",\n   161→  role: \"user\",\n   162→  content: [\n   163→    { type: \"text\", text: \"What's in this image?\" },\n   164→    { type: \"image\", url: \"https://example.com/image.png\" }\n   165→  ]\n   166→}\n   167→```\n   168→\n   169→## Best Practices\n   170→\n   171→1. **Unique IDs** - Always use unique message IDs for tracking\n   172→2. **Tool Call References** - Tool messages must reference the correct `toolCallId`\n   173→3. **Streaming** - Use streaming for long responses to improve UX\n   174→4. **Snapshots** - Use snapshots for initialization, streaming for updates\n   175→\n   176→## Source\n   177→\n   178→https://github.com/ag-ui-protocol/ag-ui/blob/main/docs/concepts/messages.mdx\n   179→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n",
